<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Egypt under Muhammad Ali</h1><p class="st-meta">1805 CE – 1867 CE · capital Cairo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Egypt transformed by Muhammad Ali and his heirs into a modernising, expansionist power, nominally Ottoman but effectively independent.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali</a>, an Albanian officer sent to fight the French, exploited the chaos after the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-occupation-of-egypt">French occupation of Egypt</a> and was recognised as Ottoman governor in 1805. He destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> leadership in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo-citadel">Cairo Citadel</a> massacre of 1811.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>He built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscript</a> army and navy, factories and schools, sent students to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> a state-controlled export. His armies fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wahhabism">Wahhabis</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> in 1820 to 1821 and, under his son Ibrahim, invaded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> in 1831. The European powers forced him back in 1840 but granted his family hereditary rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-pasha">Said Pasha</a> granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> concession in 1854.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1867 the Ottoman sultan granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha">Ismail Pasha</a> the title of khedive, turning the province into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/khedivate-of-egypt">Khedivate of Egypt</a>. The dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> until 1953, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali</a>'s reforms laid the foundations of the modern Egyptian state, army and administration.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/egypt-under-muhammad-ali.jpg" alt="Egypt under Muhammad Ali" width="320" height="446"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Kingdom_of_Egypt_(blue_color).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1805 CE – 1867 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cairo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad Ali, Ibrahim Pasha, Said Pasha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Ottoman Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a href="/countries/sudan">Sudan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Muhammad Ali dynasty, Eyalet of Egypt (1805-1867)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1836&amp;focus=egypt-under-muhammad-ali" aria-label="Egypt under Muhammad Ali on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5491 1081 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Egypt under Muhammad Ali lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5811 1337L5811 1337M5811 1337L5709 970M5811 1337L5811 1337M5811 1337L5883 1851M5811 1337L5862 1749M5811 1337L5888 1853"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5811 1337h0M5709 970h0M5811 1337h0M5883 1851h0M5862 1749h0M5888 1853h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5811 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5811 1337h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cairo. <a href="/atlas?year=1836&amp;focus=egypt-under-muhammad-ali">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
